{"version":"6.9","hitCount":894,"nextCursorMark":"AoIIQHc2Tyg0OTc5OTk5MQ==","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=%28%28TITLE%3A%22CRISPR-CAS9%22+OR+ABSTRACT%3A%22CRISPR-CAS9%22%29+AND+%28TITLE%3Asince+OR+ABSTRACT%3Asince%29%29+AND+PUB_YEAR%3A%5B2020+TO+2027%5D&cursorMark=AoIIQHc2Tyg0OTc5OTk5MQ%3D%3D&resultType=core&pageSize=50&format=json","request":{"queryString":"((TITLE:\"CRISPR-CAS9\" OR ABSTRACT:\"CRISPR-CAS9\") AND (TITLE:since OR ABSTRACT:since)) AND PUB_YEAR:[2020 TO 2027]","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":50,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"PPR1338146","source":"PPR","doi":"10.64898/2026.10.08.756992","title":"A quick and efficient method enabling lncRNA functional studies in the marine diatom  <i>Phaeodactylum tricornutum</i>  using a multiplexed CRISPR-Cas9 editing approach","authorString":"Charton F, Revillon N, Pierre-Elies P, Matsui H, Bowler C, Matsuda Y, Cruz de Carvalho H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Charton F","lastName":"Charton","firstName":"Florent","initials":"F"},{"fullName":"Revillon N","lastName":"Revillon","firstName":"Neo","initials":"N"},{"fullName":"Pierre-Elies P","lastName":"Pierre-Elies","firstName":"Priscillia","initials":"P"},{"fullName":"Matsui H","lastName":"Matsui","firstName":"Hiroaki","initials":"H"},{"fullName":"Bowler C","lastName":"Bowler","firstName":"Chris","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Matsuda Y","lastName":"Matsuda","firstName":"Yusuke","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Cruz de Carvalho H","lastName":"Cruz de Carvalho","firstName":"Helena","initials":"H"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>  Long non protein coding (lnc)RNAs are ubiquitous transcripts known to be major regulators of cellular processes in eukaryotes. In diatoms, specific lncRNA expression is tightly correlated to nutrient fluctuations, which suggests function, but their biological relevance remains to be established. In order to fill this knowledge gap, we sought to design a protocol to generate diatom lncRNA knockout mutants by CRISPR-Cas9 enabling subsequent functional studies to shed light into their mechanistic roles. Since lncRNAs do not contain open reading frames that can be targeted by point mutations for the generation of functional knockouts, we designed a multiplexed CRISPR-Cas9 plasmid harboring two independent gRNA for the specific ablation of lncRNA loci in  P. tricornutum . As proof of concept, we used our plasmid-based system to target three lncRNAs whose expression is known to be responsive to phosphate depletion.  <h4>Results</h4>  Using the golden gate based uLoop system, we assembled a single plasmid containing all the necessary components to generate CRISPR-Cas9 large genomic ablations of lncRNAs in  P. tricornutum using bacterial conjugation. Three intergenic lncRNAs (  Linc91 ,  Linc77 and  Linc24 ) responsive to phosphate fluctuations were selected and independently targeted. Around 32% of the exconjugants presented a monoallelic ablation of the targeted loci and 11% presented a biallelic ablation on the first PCR-screening round. Mutated lines, harboring biallelic lncRNAs ablations for each lncRNA targeted, were successfully generated for each of the targeted lncRNAs. Additionally, a first line of phenotypic analysis revealed significant growth rate differences for one of the mutants (  Δlinc24 ) under normal and phosphate depleted conditions, suggesting its involvement in cell division regulation under nutrient fluctuations.  <h4>Conclusions</h4>  This work provides a simple and efficient method for the generation of lncRNA knock-out mutants in  P. tricornutum . Furthermore, this approach can be easily adapted to other microalgae amenable to transformation, enabling the enhancement of our understanding of these important cellular regulatory transcripts in the marine environment.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"bioRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Agence Nationale de la Recherche","grantId":"ANR-10-IDEX-0001","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Japanese","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Agence Nationale de la Recherche","grantId":"ANR DiaLincs 19-CE43-0011-01","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Campus France","grantId":"PHC-Sakura 53278SF","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Agence Nationale de la Recherche","grantId":"ANR-23-CE20-0048-01","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.10.08.756992"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-10","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41028476","source":"MED","pmid":"41028476","doi":"10.1007/978-1-0716-4779-0_25","title":"Engineering Pichia pastoris Strains Using CRISPR/Cas9 Technologies: The Basic Protocol.","authorString":"Raschmanová H, Weninger A, Kovar K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Raschmanová H","lastName":"Raschmanová","firstName":"Hana","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Chemistry and Technology Prague, Department of Biotechnology, Prague, Czech Republic."}]}},{"fullName":"Weninger A","lastName":"Weninger","firstName":"Astrid","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graz University of Technology, Institute of Molecular Biotechnology, Graz, Austria."}]}},{"fullName":"Kovar K","lastName":"Kovar","firstName":"Karin","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"daspool, Wädenswil, Switzerland."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"2697","journalIssueId":4024143,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Methods Mol Biol","title":"Methods in molecular biology (Clifton, N.J.)","essn":"1940-6029","issn":"1064-3745","nlmid":"9214969","isoabbreviation":"Methods Mol Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"361-371","abstractText":"The CRISPR/Cas9 (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats, CRISPR-associated protein 9) system has become a commonly used tool for genome editing and metabolic engineering. For Komagataella phaffii, commercialized as Pichia pastoris, the CRISPR/Cas9 protocol for genome editing was established in 2016 and since then has been employed to facilitate genetic modifications such as markerless gene disruptions and deletions as well as to enhance the efficiency of homologous recombination.In this chapter, we describe a robust basic protocol for CRISPR-based genome editing, demonstrating near 100% targeting efficiency for gene inactivation via a frameshift mutation. As described in other chapters of this volume, CRISPR/Cas9 technologies for use in P. pastoris have been further optimized for various specific purposes.","affiliation":"University of Chemistry and Technology Prague, Department of Biotechnology, Prague, Czech Republic.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Saccharomycetales","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Pichia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genome, Fungal"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Metabolic Engineering","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"RNA, Guide, CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Pichia pastoris","Gene knockout","Homologous Recombination","Genome Engineering","Synthetic Biology","Genome Editing","Crispr/cas9","Guide Rna","Komagataella Phaffii"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"RNA, Guide, CRISPR-Cas Systems","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-0716-4779-0_25"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2025-10-01","dateOfCreation":"2025-10-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","firstIndexDate":"2025-10-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41523104","source":"MED","pmid":"41523104","pmcid":"PMC12782847","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12782847"]},"doi":"10.21769/bioprotoc.5562","title":"Creating Loss-of-Function Mutants of &lt;i&gt;Neurospora crassa&lt;/i&gt; Using a Novel CRISPR/Cas9 System.","authorString":"Grüttner S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Grüttner S","lastName":"Grüttner","firstName":"Stefanie","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Abteilung Botanische Genetik und Molekularbiologie, Botanisches Institut und Botanischer Garten, Christian-Albrechts-Universität zu Kiel, Kiel, Germany."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4089623,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Bio Protoc","title":"Bio-protocol","essn":"2331-8325","issn":"2331-8325","nlmid":"101635102","isoabbreviation":"Bio Protoc"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e5562","abstractText":"Since its introduction, the CRISPR/Cas9 system has been used in many organisms for precise and rapid genome editing, as well as for editing multiple genes at once. This targeted mutagenesis makes it easy to analyze the function of a gene of interest (goi). The standard method for genetic manipulation of the model organism <i>Neurospora crassa</i> has been homologous recombination. It is well established and widely used to create knock-out or overexpression mutants. The recently developed CRISPR/Cas9 system is an addition to the toolkit for genetically manipulating <i>N. crassa</i>. For this protocol, a strain stably expressing the Cas9 endonuclease is required. After designing the gRNA with the online tool CHOP-CHOP, a synthetic gRNA is used to transform macroconidia via electroporation. Combining the goi-gRNA with a gRNA targeting the <i>csr-1</i> gene as a selection marker allows for easy identification of colonies with mutations at the target site of the goi, since the obtained resistance to Cyclosporin A (CsA) allows for selecting editing events. The mutation type can be detected by PCR of the edited gene region followed by Sanger sequencing. This system is fast and easy to handle, offering an attractive alternative to homologous recombination, especially for targeting multiple genes simultaneously. Key features • This protocol allows the use of CRISPR/Cas9 in <i>Neurospora crassa</i> to create loss-of-function mutants. • It can be used to create loss-of-function mutants of multiple genes in one round of transformation. • Time-saving mutagenesis without the need for vector construction. • In combination with <i>csr-1</i> as a selection marker, the screening for successfully edited genes of interest is reduced.","affiliation":"Abteilung Botanische Genetik und Molekularbiologie, Botanisches Institut und Botanischer Garten, Christian-Albrechts-Universität zu Kiel, Kiel, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["methods-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Electroporation","neurospora crassa","Targeted mutagenesis","Genome Editing","Crispr/cas9","Loss-of-function Mutagenesis","Csr-1 Selection Marker"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21769/BioProtoc.5562"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12782847"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12782847?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-12","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-14","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41387770","source":"MED","pmid":"41387770","pmcid":"PMC12821853","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12821853"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12870-025-07429-2","title":"Callus and endosperm green fluorescence reporter-assisted selection system in maize CRISPR/Cas9 gene editing.","authorString":"Ge L, Li W, Dou Y, Ma Y, Sun M, Chen X, Feng X, Li Y, Yu Q.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ge L","lastName":"Ge","firstName":"Lei","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Characteristic Laboratory of Crop Germplasm Innovation and Application, Provincial Department of Education, College of Agronomy, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China."},{"affiliation":"Academician Workstation of Agricultural High-tech Industrial Area of the Yellow River Delta, National Center of Technology Innovation for Comprehensive Utilization of Saline-Alkali Land, Dongying, Shandong, 257300, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li W","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Wenbo","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Characteristic Laboratory of Crop Germplasm Innovation and Application, Provincial Department of Education, College of Agronomy, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Dou Y","lastName":"Dou","firstName":"Yiming","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Characteristic Laboratory of Crop Germplasm Innovation and Application, Provincial Department of Education, College of Agronomy, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ma Y","lastName":"Ma","firstName":"Yu","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Characteristic Laboratory of Crop Germplasm Innovation and Application, Provincial Department of Education, College of Agronomy, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun M","lastName":"Sun","firstName":"Mengyao","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Characteristic Laboratory of Crop Germplasm Innovation and Application, Provincial Department of Education, College of Agronomy, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen X","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Xiaoya","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Characteristic Laboratory of Crop Germplasm Innovation and Application, Provincial Department of Education, College of Agronomy, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Feng X","lastName":"Feng","firstName":"Xue","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Characteristic Laboratory of Crop Germplasm Innovation and Application, Provincial Department of Education, College of Agronomy, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li Y","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Yubin","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Life Science, Shanxi Normal University, Taiyuan, 030031, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yu Q","lastName":"Yu","firstName":"Qian","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Characteristic Laboratory of Crop Germplasm Innovation and Application, Provincial Department of Education, College of Agronomy, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China. yuqian@qau.edu.cn."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4083234,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Plant Biol","title":"BMC plant biology","essn":"1471-2229","issn":"1471-2229","nlmid":"100967807","isoabbreviation":"BMC Plant Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-12-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"92","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Genome editing using CRISPR/Cas9 (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats/CRISPR-associated protein 9) has emerged as a promising approach for functional gene analysis and genetic improvement. Since stable transformation remains the primary method for implementing this system, the ultimate goal in crop breeding programs would require the selection of transgene-free plants with the CRISPR/Cas expression cassette removed.<h4>Results</h4>In this study, we developed an endosperm-specific fluorescence reporter-assisted selection system for CRISPR/Cas9 gene editing (pAZS22-eGFP/CRISPR/Cas9) in maize (Zea mays L.), utilizing enhanced green fluorescent protein (eGFP) expressed specifically in the endosperm to facilitate the easy identification of transgenic and transgene-free plants from the T<sub>1</sub> generation on. In addition, the 22 kDa alpha zein (z1C1_10) promoter from maize, employed in this system, has been shown to be active in both callus and endosperm, thereby being able to enhance the accuracy of transformant identification during the tissue culture process by reducing false positives compared to the traditional selective media methods. Our studies targeting the ZmSnRK2.1 or Dwarf1 (D1) genes demonstrated a reasonable editing efficiency, with rates ranging from 56.3% for T<sub>0</sub> plants targeting ZmSnRK2.1, to 87.5% and 100% for T<sub>1</sub> plants targeting D1 and ZmSnRK2.1, respectively. In addition, we successfully identified 1 transgene-free homozygous d1 mutant in the T<sub>1</sub> generation and 7 transgene-free homozygous snrk2.1 mutants in the T<sub>2</sub> generation.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The pAZS22-eGFP/CRISPR/Cas9 system provides an efficient tool for gene editing, transformant selection and transgene status identification in maize breeding.","affiliation":"The Characteristic Laboratory of Crop Germplasm Innovation and Application, Provincial Department of Education, College of Agronomy, Qingdao Agricultural University, Qingdao, 266109, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","