ailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology, Cornell University, Ithaca, NY, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Venkataraman S","lastName":"Venkataraman","firstName":"Srividhya","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cell & Systems Biology, University of Toronto, Toronto, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Alok A","lastName":"Alok","firstName":"Anshu","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0733-9117"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant pathology, University of Minnesota, Saint Paul, MN, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Moiketsi BN","lastName":"Moiketsi","firstName":"Bertha N","initials":"BN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences & Biotechnology, Botswana International University of Sciences and Technology, Palapye, Botswana."}]}},{"fullName":"Malik S","lastName":"Malik","firstName":"Sonia","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Physiology, Ecology and Environment (P2E) laboratory, University of Orleans, INRAE, Orleans, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Masisi K","lastName":"Masisi","firstName":"Kabo","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences & Biotechnology, Botswana International University of Sciences and Technology, Palapye, Botswana."}]}},{"fullName":"Rantong G","lastName":"Rantong","firstName":"Gaolathe","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences & Biotechnology, Botswana International University of Sciences and Technology, Palapye, Botswana."}]}},{"fullName":"Kwape T","lastName":"Kwape","firstName":"Tebogo","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences & Biotechnology, Botswana International University of Sciences and Technology, Palapye, Botswana."}]}},{"fullName":"Gaobotse G","lastName":"Gaobotse","firstName":"Goabaone","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2174-2823"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences & Biotechnology, Botswana International University of Sciences and Technology, Palapye, Botswana."}]}},{"fullName":"Makhzoum A","lastName":"Makhzoum","firstName":"Abdullah","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences & Biotechnology, Botswana International University of Sciences and Technology, Palapye, Botswana."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0733-9117"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2174-2823"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1-3","volume":"61","journalIssueId":4190022,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb-Feb","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Crit Rev Biochem Mol Biol","title":"Critical reviews in biochemistry and molecular biology","essn":"1549-7798","issn":"1040-9238","nlmid":"8903774","isoabbreviation":"Crit Rev Biochem Mol Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1-16","abstractText":"Plants have been a part of human health since our very beginnings, and many of our modern pharmaceuticals claim their origins from medicinal plants. The range of specialized metabolites synthesized by plants is highly diverse, and metabolic functions have developed over the millennia to cover roles such as defense, adaptation to environmental stress, and even reproduction. These metabolites subsequently play roles in human health and diseases that are both significant and profound. The importance of plant natural products for the pharmaceutical, cosmetic and nutraceutical industries cannot be overstated. However, the fact that these specialized metabolites may be available only in low quantities from plants that are slow growing, endangered, or from fragile environments due to certain biotic and abiotic stresses makes their commercial use challenging despite the scenario that some stresses can enhance the production of secondary metabolites. Genome editing is a technique or technology that comprises of tools like CRISPR/Cas9, TALEN, ZFN. The following review describes the successful use of CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing in engineering medicinal plants, food crops and commercial crops to modulate metabolic pathways involved in the biosynthesis of valuable compounds to improve natural product identification, development and ultimately, commercial viability.","affiliation":"Department of Microbiology, Cornell University, Ithaca, NY, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Plants, Genetically Modified","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Crops, Agricultural","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Plants, Medicinal","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Biological Products","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Medicinal plants","Human disease","Natural products","Genome Editing","Environmental Sustainability"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Biological Products","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/10409238.2025.2577956"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-06","dateOfCreation":"2025-12-25","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-14","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-25","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-25","firstIndexDate":"2025-12-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41585947","source":"MED","pmid":"41585947","pmcid":"PMC12827552","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12827552"]},"doi":"10.3389/fgeed.2025.1729535","title":"CRISPR/Cas9-based programmable genome editing in chickens: concepts, applications and regulatory issues.","authorString":"Kolluri G, Naim A, Kumar Kurva S, Tyagi JS, Ansari MM, Tomar S, Tiwari AK, Chouhan L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kolluri G","lastName":"Kolluri","firstName":"Gautham","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Molecular Physiology Laboratory, Division of Avian Physiology and Reproduction, ICAR-Central Avian Research Institute, Izatnagar, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Naim A","lastName":"Naim","firstName":"Adnan","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Molecular Physiology Laboratory, Division of Avian Physiology and Reproduction, ICAR-Central Avian Research Institute, Izatnagar, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar Kurva S","lastName":"Kumar Kurva","firstName":"Shiva","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Molecular Physiology Laboratory, Division of Avian Physiology and Reproduction, ICAR-Central Avian Research Institute, Izatnagar, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Tyagi JS","lastName":"Tyagi","firstName":"Jagbir Singh","initials":"JS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Molecular Physiology Laboratory, Division of Avian Physiology and Reproduction, ICAR-Central Avian Research Institute, Izatnagar, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Ansari MM","lastName":"Ansari","firstName":"Mohd Matin","initials":"MM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Molecular Physiology Laboratory, Division of Avian Physiology and Reproduction, ICAR-Central Avian Research Institute, Izatnagar, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Tomar S","lastName":"Tomar","firstName":"Simmi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7517-7650"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Avian Genetics and Breeding, ICAR-Central Avian Research Institute, Izatnagar, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Tiwari AK","lastName":"Tiwari","firstName":"Ashok Kumar","initials":"AK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Molecular Physiology Laboratory, Division of Avian Physiology and Reproduction, ICAR-Central Avian Research Institute, Izatnagar, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Chouhan L","lastName":"Chouhan","firstName":"Laxmi","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Poultry Science, Veterinary College, Jabalpur, Madhya Pradesh, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7517-7650"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"7","journalIssueId":3899875,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","title":"Frontiers in genome editing","essn":"2673-3439","issn":"2673-3439","nlmid":"101775540","isoabbreviation":"Front Genome Ed"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"1729535","abstractText":"The advent of genetics, molecular biology, and genome sequencing has rapidly accelerated the development of elite genetic lines across various species, including poultry. It is now possible to introduce intra- or inter-species single nucleotide polymorphisms into chicken lines to enhance productivity. This advancement may mark the beginning of a new agricultural revolution, dramatically reducing the time required to improve poultry lines for commercial production environments. Transgenic technologies, including lentiviral vectors and piggyBac transposition, have enabled the generation of animals expressing exogenous genes. The emergence of new genome-editing tools is transforming avian biotechnology, allowing the creation of customized organisms for specific traits. Genome editing has become the most efficient method for studying gene function. First and second generation tools, such as zinc finger nucleases and transcription activator-like effector nucleases (TALENs), are limited by complex design and off-target effects. In contrast, the third generation Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats/CRISPR-associated protein 9 (CRISPR/Cas9), represents a significant breakthrough. It encompasses guided RNA (gRNA) and the Cas9 endonuclease which together target specific DNA sequences and induces double-strand breaks that are repaired <i>via</i> error-prone non-homologous end joining, frequently causing insertions or deletions that disrupt gene function. Targeting specificity is achieved through gRNA-DNA base pairing and recognition of a protospacer adjacent motif by Cas9. Beyond gene knockout, CRISPR/Cas9 enables functional analysis of non-coding elements such as enhancers and insulators. Delivered <i>via</i> plasmid systems, Cas9 and gRNA are transiently expressed and degrade within 48-72 h, leaving no permanent genetic footprint. Since no exogenous DNA is integrated, this approach is generally considered less contentious than traditional transgenic methods in the context of genetically modified organism regulation. CRISPR/Cas9 has diverse applications in poultry, including enhancing disease resistance to avian influenza and Marek's disease, improving productivity traits such as growth, feed efficiency, and egg-laying, and enabling early in-ovo sexing to address ethical concerns around male chick culling. It also allows control of reproductive traits for breeding management, supports bio-pharming by producing therapeutic proteins or vaccines in eggs, and facilitates functional genomics through precise gene knockouts to study development, immunity, and metabolism.","affiliation":"Molecular Physiology Laboratory, Division of Avian Physiology and Reproduction, ICAR-Central Avian Research Institute, Izatnagar, India.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Gene knockout","Chicken","avian influenza","Genome Editing","Crispr/cas9","Bio-pharming"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fgeed.2025.1729535"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12827552"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12827552?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":5,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-24","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41607697","source":"MED","pmid":"41607697","pmcid":"PMC12835662","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12835662"]},"doi":"10.21769/bioprotoc.5572","title":"<i>Plasmodium berghei</i> High-Throughput (PbHiT): a CRISPR-Cas9 System to Study Genes at Scale.","authorString":"Jonsdottir TK, Paoletta MS, Henriksson J, Bushell ESC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Jonsdottir TK","lastName":"Jonsdottir","firstName":"Thorey K","initials":"TK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Laboratory for Molecular Infection Medicine Sweden, Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biology, Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Paoletta MS","lastName":"Paoletta","firstName":"Martina S","initials":"MS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Laboratory for Molecular Infection Medicine Sweden, Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biology, Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Instituto de Agrobiotecnología y Biología Molecular (IABIMO), INTA-CONICET, Hurlingham, Buenos Aires, Argentina."}]}},{"fullName":"Henriksson J","lastName":"Henriksson","firstName":"Johan","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biology, Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"IceLab, Umeå University, Naturvetarhuset, Universitetsvägen, 901 87 Umeå, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Umeå Center for Microbial Research (UCMR), Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"SciLifeLab, Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Bushell ESC","lastName":"Bushell","firstName":"Ellen S C","initials":"ESC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Laboratory for Molecular Infection Medicine Sweden, Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biology, Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"Umeå Center for Microbial Research (UCMR), Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4104134,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Bio Protoc","title":"Bio-protocol","essn":"2331-8325","issn":"2331-8325","nlmid":"101635102","isoabbreviation":"Bio Protoc"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e5572","abstractText":"Genetic modification is essential for understanding parasite biology, yet it remains challenging in <i>Plasmodium.</i> This is partially due to the parasite's low genetic tractability and reliance on homologous recombination, since the parasites lack the canonical non-homologous end-joining pathway. Existing approaches, such as the <i>Plasmo</i>GEM project, enable genome-wide knockouts but remain limited in coverage and flexibility. Here, we present the <i>Plasmodium berghei</i> high-throughput (PbHiT) system, a scalable CRISPR-Cas9 protocol for efficient genome editing in rodent malaria parasites. The PbHiT method uses a single cloning step to generate vectors in which a guide RNA (gRNA) is physically linked to short (100 bp) homology arms, enabling precise integration at the target locus upon transfection. The gRNA also serves as a unique barcode, allowing pooled vector transfections and identification of mutants by downstream gRNA sequencing. The PbHiT system reliably recapitulates known mutant growth phenotypes and supports both knockout and tagging strategies. This protocol provides a reproducible and scalable tool for genome editing in <i>P. berghei</i>, enabling both targeted functional studies and high-throughput genetic screens. Additionally, we provide an online resource covering the entire <i>P. berghei</i> protein-coding genome and describe a step-by-step pooled ligation approach for large-scale vector production. Key features • PbHiT provides a high-throughput CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing platform optimised for <i>Plasmodium berghei</i> experimental infections in rodents. • This protocol enables efficient and reproducible generation of knockout and tagged parasite lines using short homology arms. • This protocol is supported by a free online resource for <i>P. berghei</i> gene construct design and requires basic knowledge of cloning. • Transfection of <i>Plasmodium berghei</i> requires experience in handling mice/rats, an ethical permit, and an animal facility.","affiliation":"The Laboratory for Molecular Infection Medicine Sweden, Umeå University, Umeå, Sweden.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["methods-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Plasmodium","Transfection","Malaria","Gene modification","High-throughput","Plasmodium Berghei","Crispr-cas9","Apicomplexan"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21769/BioProtoc.5572"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12835662"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12835662?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gen","uniprot","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-28","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41611487","source":"MED","pmid":"41611487","doi":"10.13345/j.cjb.250419","title":"[Research progress and prospects in CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing in sorghum].","authorString":"Huang X, Zeng G, Shen X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Huang X","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Xiuxiu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Innovation Team for Sorghum Functional Genomics and Circular Agricultural Industry, China Three Gorges University, Yichang 443002, Hubei, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zeng G","lastName":"Zeng","firstName":"Gongjian","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Innovation Team for Sorghum Functional Genomics and Circular Agricultural Industry, China Three Gorges University, Yichang 443002, Hubei, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shen X","lastName":"Shen","firstName":"Xiangling","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Innovation Team for Sorghum Functional Genomics and Circular Agricultural Industry, China Three Gorges University, Yichang 443002, Hubei, China. shenxl@ctg.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"42","journalIssueId":4110686,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Sheng Wu Gong Cheng Xue Bao","title":"Sheng wu gong cheng xue bao = Chinese journal of biotechnology","essn":"1872-2075","issn":"1000-3061","nlmid":"9426463","isoabbreviation":"Sheng Wu Gong Cheng Xue Bao"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"53-61","abstractText":"Sorghum is an important grain and cash crop in China, and the promotion and application of this crop have long been constrained by a shortage of genetic resources. Issues such as narrow genetic background and outdated breeding techniques have severely hindered the development and dissemination of new sorghum varieties. Although gene editing has demonstrated significant potential in the genetic improvement of crops since its inception, the application of this technology in sorghum remains lagging. This paper provides a systematic review of the latest breakthroughs in CRISPR-Cas9 in sorghum. Focusing on abiotic stress, growth and development, and quality, we explore the innovative applications of this technology in expanding genetic diversity, improving stress tolerance, optimizing plant architecture and yield potential, and enhancing quality characteristics. Additionally, we analyze the main technical challenges including low genetic transformation efficiency and insufficient adaptability of editing tools facing the gene editing in sorghum. Finally, we make an outlook on the future prospects of next-generation gene editing technologies in the genetic improvement of sorghum. This paper can provide important theoretical references for sorghum molecular breeding, and hold significant practical significance for safeguarding China's food security and enhancing the competitiveness of the sorghum industry.","affiliation":"Innovation Team for Sorghum Functional Genomics and Circular Agricultural Industry, China Three Gorges University, Yichang 443002, Hubei, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"chi","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","Review","Journal Article","English Abstract"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"the Innovation-driven Assistance Project of China Association for Science and Technology","grantId":"