Kévin","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1837-1055"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IRF (Infections Respiratoires Fongiques), Univ Angers, Univ Brest, SFR ICAT 4208, Angers, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Poirier W","lastName":"Poirier","firstName":"Wilfried","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IRF (Infections Respiratoires Fongiques), Univ Angers, Univ Brest, SFR ICAT 4208, Angers, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Razafimandimby B","lastName":"Razafimandimby","firstName":"Bienvenue","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IRF (Infections Respiratoires Fongiques), Univ Angers, Univ Brest, SFR ICAT 4208, Angers, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Bouchara JP","lastName":"Bouchara","firstName":"Jean-Philippe","initials":"JP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IRF (Infections Respiratoires Fongiques), Univ Angers, Univ Brest, SFR ICAT 4208, Angers, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Gastebois A","lastName":"Gastebois","firstName":"Amandine","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IRF (Infections Respiratoires Fongiques), Univ Angers, Univ Brest, SFR ICAT 4208, Angers, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Giraud S","lastName":"Giraud","firstName":"Sandrine","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3214-1719"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IRF (Infections Respiratoires Fongiques), Univ Angers, Univ Brest, SFR ICAT 4208, Angers, France. sandrine.giraud@univ-angers.fr."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3214-1719"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1837-1055"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"190","journalIssueId":4020530,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mycopathologia","title":"Mycopathologia","essn":"1573-0832","issn":"0301-486X","nlmid":"7505689","isoabbreviation":"Mycopathologia"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-09-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"94","abstractText":"Scedosporium species are opportunistic pathogens causing a large variety of human infections. To date, there is limited information on the pathogenic mechanisms of these fungi, partly because of the limited number of genetic tools available. Here, the CRISPR-Cas9 technology, which provided promising results for functional genomic studies in filamentous fungi, was optimized for Scedosporium species using in vitro assembled Cas9 ribonucleoprotein (RNP) complexes. In these fungi, functional genomic studies are particularly complex in a wild-type strain, because of the high frequency of non-homologous recombination. Prior disruption of the KU70 gene encoding one of the components of the non-homologous end joining system is required, which necessitates the use of a first selection marker. The cleavage of the target gene at each end using a dual RNA-guided Cas9 complex, followed by recombination with a repair template containing the hygromycin resistance gene, allowed disruption of the target gene in the ΔKU70 mutant. Four genes encoding dioxygenases, catalyzing the critical ring-opening step in aromatic hydrocarbons, were successfully disrupted, and the optimum efficiency was observed using 5 μg of the HygR repair cassette. Alternatively, in the wild-type strain, the exclusive use of two Cas9 RNP complexes was enough to achieve an efficient deletion method; one dioxygenase gene was successfully deleted in up to 20% of the obtained colonies. These last experimental conditions path the way to multiple gene deletions and complementation experiments, which cannot be reached using our first procedure since only two selection markers are available for Scedosporium species.","affiliation":"IRF (Infections Respiratoires Fongiques), Univ Angers, Univ Brest, SFR ICAT 4208, Angers, France.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Scedosporium","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Optimization","Scedosporium Apiospermum","Crispr-cas9","In Vitro Strategy","Fungal Genome Editing"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11046-025-00998-8"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2025-09-30","dateOfCreation":"2025-09-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-09-30","firstPublicationDate":"2025-09-30","firstIndexDate":"2025-10-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42649271","source":"MED","pmid":"42649271","doi":"10.1038/s41434-026-00641-1","title":"Mapping the global trends and hotspots of research on gene therapy and hair cell regeneration for hearing loss: a comprehensive data-mining-based study.","authorString":"Liu Y, Hu B, Zhang J, Wu X, Lu Q, Jia Y, Kou Z.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liu Y","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Yunsheng","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cancer Center, Shenzhen Hospital (Futian) of Guangzhou University of Chinese Medicine, Shenzhen, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu B","lastName":"Hu","firstName":"Bing","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology, Jiangxi Provincial People's Hospital, the First Affiliated Hospital of Nanchang Medical College, Nanchang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang J","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Jinfang","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cancer Center, Shenzhen Hospital (Futian) of Guangzhou University of Chinese Medicine, Shenzhen, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu X","lastName":"Wu","firstName":"Xianlin","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cancer Center, Shenzhen Hospital (Futian) of Guangzhou University of Chinese Medicine, Shenzhen, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lu Q","lastName":"Lu","firstName":"Qinghui","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shenzhen Hospital (Futian) of Guangzhou University of Chinese Medicine, Shenzhen, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Jia Y","lastName":"Jia","firstName":"Yanyan","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7089-0959"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ENT Institute and Otorhinolaryngology Department of Eye & ENT Hospital, State Key Laboratory of Medical Neurobiology and MOE Frontiers Center for Brain Science, Fudan University, Shanghai, China. jiayanyan@fudan.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Kou Z","lastName":"Kou","firstName":"Zengwei","initials":"Z","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-8779-7186"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Laboratory Medicine and Pathobiology, Temerty Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada. zengwei.kou@utoronto.ca."},{"affiliation":"College of Basic Medicine and Forensic Medicine, Henan University of Science and Technology, Luoyang, China. zengwei.kou@utoronto.ca."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7089-0959"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-8779-7186"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4240178,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Gene Ther","title":"Gene therapy","essn":"1476-5462","issn":"0969-7128","nlmid":"9421525","isoabbreviation":"Gene Ther"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Hearing loss poses substantial global healthcare challenges, and conventional treatments remain inadequate for genetic etiologies, necessitating innovative approaches like gene therapy. This study presents the first comprehensive bibliometric analysis of hearing loss gene therapy to systematically map global trends, key contributors, and technological breakthroughs. We analyzed 2913 publications (1993-2025) from Web of Science, PubMed, and Scopus using advanced tools like CiteSpace, VOSviewer, and Bibliometrix. Metrics encompassed publication trends, country and institutional contributions, journal influence, author productivity, and keyword networks, with a focus on adeno-associated virus vectors and CRISPR/Cas9 technologies. Results indicate an exponential growth in publications, driven primarily by the USA and China, with the USA maintaining the highest citation impact. Harvard University, the University of Michigan, and Fudan University emerged as the top contributing institutions. Keyword analysis identified AAV-driven hair cell regeneration and CRISPR/Cas9-mediated mutation correction as the dominant research frontiers. While AAV-based studies currently dominate highly cited works, CRISPR/Cas9 applications have gained significant momentum since 2015. Furthermore, clinical trials targeting genes like OTOF reflect rapid translational progress, with round-window membrane injection emerging as the most prevalent and promising surgical delivery approach. Ultimately, this study highlights the dynamic evolution of auditory gene therapy, providing a roadmap that bridges foundational discoveries with clinical applications while underscoring the necessity of global collaboration.","affiliation":"Cancer Center, Shenzhen Hospital (Futian) of Guangzhou University of Chinese Medicine, Shenzhen, China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Shenzhen Natural Science Foundation Program","grantId":"JCYJ20230807120459011","orderIn":0},{"agency":"the National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"32100762","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41434-026-00641-1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-27","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR994605","source":"PPR","doi":"10.1101/2025.03.23.644600","title":"Selection-free CRISPR-Cas9 editing protocol for distant Dictyostelid species","authorString":"Garriga-Canut M, Cannon N, Benton M, Zanon A, Horsfield ST, Scheurich J, Remans K, Lees J, Paix A, van Gestel J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Garriga-Canut M","lastName":"Garriga-Canut","firstName":"Mireia","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Cannon N","lastName":"Cannon","firstName":"Nikki","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7678-8146"}},{"fullName":"Benton M","lastName":"Benton","firstName":"Matthew","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7953-0765"}},{"fullName":"Zanon A","lastName":"Zanon","firstName":"Andrea","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9261-5234"}},{"fullName":"Horsfield ST","lastName":"Horsfield","firstName":"Samuel","initials":"ST","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3859-4073"}},{"fullName":"Scheurich J","lastName":"Scheurich","firstName":"Jacob","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5982-7844"}},{"fullName":"Remans K","lastName":"Remans","firstName":"Kim","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4394-7953"}},{"fullName":"Lees J","lastName":"Lees","firstName":"John","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Paix A","lastName":"Paix","firstName":"Alexandre","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8080-7546"}},{"fullName":"van Gestel J","lastName":"van Gestel","firstName":"Jordi","initials":"J"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5982-7844"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7678-8146"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7953-0765"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3859-4073"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4394-7953"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8080-7546"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9261-5234"}]},"pubYear":"2025","abstractText":"Dictyostelids are a species-rich clade of cellular slime molds that are widely found in soils and have been studied for over a century. Most research focusses on Dictyostelium discoideum, which - due to its ease of culturing and genetic tractability - has been adopted as a model species in the fields of developmental biology, cell biology and microbiology. Over decades, genome editing methods in D. discoideum have steadily improved but remain relatively time-consuming and limited in scope, effective in a few species only. Here, we introduce a CRISPR-Cas9 editing protocol that is cloning-free, selection-free, highly-efficient, and effective across Dictyostelid species. After optimizing our protocol in D. discoideum, we obtained knock-out efficiencies of ~80% and knock-in efficiencies of ~30% without antibiotic selection. Efficiencies depend on template concentrations, insertion sizes, homology arms and target sites. Since our protocol is selection-free, we can isolate mutants as soon as one day post-transfection, vastly expediting the generation of knock-outs, fusion proteins and expression reporters. Our protocol also makes it possible to generate several knock-in mutations simultaneously in the same cells. Boosted by cell-sorting and fluorescent microscopy, we could readily apply our CRISPR-Cas9 editing protocol to phylogenetically distant Dictyostelid species, which diverged hundreds of millions of years ago and have never been genome edited before. Our protocol therefore opens the door to performing broad-scale genetic interrogations across Dicyostelids.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"bioRxiv","yearOfPublication":2025},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.03.23.644600"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2025-03-28","firstPublicationDate":"2025-03-25","firstIndexDate":"2025-04-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42787633","source":"MED","pmid":"42787633","pmcid":"PMC13601842","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13601842"]},"doi":"10.3389/fgeed.2026.1924733","title":"Advancements in CRISPR-based &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; gene therapy for hemophilia.","authorString":"Zhang X, Xu K, Zou S, Zhu Z, Teng S, Huang P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhang X","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Xiaoyu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Key Laboratory of Pathobiology, Ministry of Education, Norman Bethune College of Medicine, Jilin University, Changchun, China."},{"affiliation":"The Third Norman Bethune Clinical College of Jilin University, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu K","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Kaidi","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Key Laboratory of Pathobiology, Ministry of Education, Norman Bethune College of Medicine, Jilin University, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zou S","lastName":"Zou","firstName":"Shihui","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, Changchun People's Hospital, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhu Z","lastName":"Zhu","firstName":"Zhiyao","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Key Laboratory of Pathobiology, Ministry of Education, Norman Bethune College of Medicine, Jilin University, Changchun, China."},{"affiliation":"The Third Norman Bethune Clinical College of Jilin University, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Teng S","lastName":"Teng","firstName":"Shuzhi","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Key Laboratory of Pathobiology, Ministry of Education, Norman Bethune College of Medicine, Jilin University, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang P","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Ping","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Key Laboratory of Pathobiology, Ministry of Education, Norman Bethune College of Medicine, Jilin University, Changchun, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4145489,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","title":"Frontiers in genome editing","essn":"2673-3439","issn":"2673-3439","nlmid":"101775540","isoabbreviation":"Front Genome Ed"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1924733","abstractText":"Hemophilia is an X-linked hereditary bleeding disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the genes encoding coagulation factors, leading to excessive bleeding and potentially being life-threatening. Currently, regular treatment for hemophilia is the infusion of recombinant blood coagulation factors. This approach is not only costly but can also give rise to complications such as the development of neutralizing antibodies (Nabs), which impede the therapeutic outcome. Hemophilia is a monogenic disease, making gene therapy a promising curative measure that includes gene addition and gene editing approaches. Adeno-associated virus (AAV) vectors have gained massive attention as the premier delivery vehicle for clinical gene therapy due to their diverse tissue tropisms dictated by the natural and engineered AAV capsids, their excellent safety profile since AAVs do not cause any known human diseases, low immunogenicity, and long-lasting gene expression. Several AAV-based gene addition therapies have been approved for hemophilia B and hemophilia A, and dozens of similar AAV-based clinical trials are underway. Gene editing technologies, like Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats (CRISPR)/CRISPR-associated protein 9 (Cas9), have shown immense potential in the treatment of genetic diseases. CRISPR/Cas9-mediated transgene integration provides critical advantages over regular gene insertion therapy by enabling site-specific, targeted genomic integration rather than random integration. Clinical trials focusing on AAV-CRISPR/Cas9-mediated knock-in of human genes represent the frontier of <i>in vivo</i> genetic medicine, and one such prominent ongoing clinical trial for hemophilia B is designed to insert the human Factor IX gene into hepatocytes. This review outlines recent advances and feasible strategies of CRISPR/Cas9-based genome editing for hemophilia therapy, highlights the potential applications of next-generation gene editing and vector delivery technologies, and offers new insights for the advantages, limitations, and future directions for these novel treatment modalities.","affiliation":"The Key Laboratory of Pathobiology, Ministry of Education, Norman Bethune College of Medicine, Jilin University, Changchun, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Gene therapy","Hemophilia","AAV","Factor VIII","Factor IX","Non-viral Vectors","Gene Editing","Crispr/cas9"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fgeed.2026.1924733"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13601842"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13601842?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-25","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"40624545","source":"MED","pmid":"40624545","pmcid":"PMC12232765","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12232765"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12983-025-00566-2","title":"Transferable approaches to CRISPR-Cas9 induced genome editing in non-model insects: a brief guide.","authorString":"Ahmed HMM, Zheng L, Hunnekuhl VS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ahmed HMM","lastName":"Ahmed","firstName":"Hassan M M","initials":"HMM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4322-2987"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Developmental Biology, Johann-Friedrich-Blumenbach Institute, GZMB, University of Göttingen, Göttingen, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Department of Crop Protection, Faculty of Agriculture - University of Khartoum, 13314, Khartoum North, Sudan."}]}},{"fullName":"Zheng L","lastName":"Zheng","firstName":"Lisha","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Evolutionary Developmental Genetics, Johann-Friedrich-Blumenbach Institute, GZMB, University of Göttingen, Göttingen, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Hunnekuhl VS","lastName":"Hunnekuhl","firstName":"Vera S","initials":"VS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9100-2161"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Evolutionary Developmental Genetics, Johann-Friedrich-Blumenbach Institute, GZMB, University of Göttingen, Göttingen, Germany. vera.terblanche@uni-goettingen.de."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4322-2987"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9100-2161"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"22","journalIssueId":3970866,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Zool","title":"Frontiers in zoology","essn":"1742-9994","issn":"1742-9994","nlmid":"101231669","isoabbreviation":"Front Zool"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-07-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"13","abstractText":"Despite the large variety of insect species with divergent morphological, developmental and physiological features questions on gene function could for a long time only be addressed in few model species. The adaption of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas system for genome editing in eukaryotic cells widened the scope of the field of functional genetics: for the first time the creation of heritable genetic changes had become possible in a very broad range of organisms. Since then, targeted genome editing using the CRISPR-Cas technology has greatly increased the possibilities for genetic manipulation in non-model insects where molecular genetic tools were little established. The technology allows for site-specific mutagenesis and germline transformation. Importantly, it can be used for the generation of gene knock-outs, and for the knock-in of transgenes and generation of gene-reporter fusions. CRISPR-Cas induced genome editing can thus be applied to address questions in basic research in various insect species and other study organisms. Notably, it can also be used in applied insect biotechnology to design new pest and vector control strategies such as gene drives and precision guided Sterile Insect Technique. However, establishing CRISPR in a new model requires several practical considerations that depend on the scientific questions and on the characteristics of the respective study organism. Therefore, this review is intended to give a literature overview on different CRISPR-Cas9 based methods that have already been established in diverse insects. After discussing some required pre-conditions of the study organism, we provide a guide through experimental considerations when planning to conduct CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing, such as the design and delivery of guide RNAs, and of Cas9 endonuclease. We discuss the use of different repair mechanisms including homology directed repair (HDR) for a defined insertion of genetic elements. Furthermore, we describe different molecular methods for genetic screening and the use of visible markers. We focus our review on experimental work in insects, but due to the ubiquitous functionality of the CRISPR-Cas system many considerations are transferable to other non-model organisms.","affiliation":"Department of Developmental Biology, Johann-Friedrich-Blumenbach Institute, GZMB, University of Göttingen, Göttingen, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grantId":"TE1380/1","orderIn":0},{"agency":"China Scholarship Council","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Georg-August-Universität Göttingen","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Insects","Genome Editing","Crispr-cas9","New Model Organisms"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12983-025-00566-2"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12232765"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12232765?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":6,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2025-07-07","dateOfRevision":"2025-07-09","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-07-07","firstPublicationDate":"2025-07-07","firstIndexDate":"2025-07-09","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1001001","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-5663569/v1","title":"A Single-Vector CRISPR/Cas9 System for Genome Editing and Heterologous Enzyme Secretion in Saccharomyces cerevisiae: A Case Study on Pectate Lyase for Coffee Mucilage Removal","authorString":"Lam LHT, Nhi NHH, Lan VTH, Hau NV, Nghia NH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lam LHT","lastName":"Lam","firstName":"La Ho Truc","initials":"LHT"},{"fullName":"Nhi NHH","lastName":"Nhi","firstName":"Nguyen Huynh Ha","initials":"NHH"},{"fullName":"Lan VTH","lastName":"Lan","firstName":"Vo Thi Hoang","initials":"VTH"},{"fullName":"Hau NV","lastName":"Hau","firstName":"Nguyen Van","initials":"NV"},{"fullName":"Nghia NH","lastName":"Nghia","firstName":"Nguyen Hieu","initials":"NH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3151-2781"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3939-4348"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3151-2781"}]},"pubYear":"2025","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>The CRISPR/Cas9 system facilitates precise genome editing in various organisms. In this study, a single-vector CRISPR/Cas9 system was developed for <italic>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</italic>, employing a type II Cas9 enzyme from <italic>Streptococcus pyogenes</italic> and a single-guide RNA cassette targeting <italic>CAN1.Y</italic> locus on chromosome V. This system is broadly applicable across yeast strains, as it utilizes G418 selection, eliminating the need for auxotrophic markers. The efficiency of the CRISPR/Cas9 system was demonstrated, with editing efficiencies ranging from 70–100%. This system was utilized to integrate a cassette encoding secretory pectate lyase (PL) from <italic>Bacillus subtilis</italic> 168 into the yeast genome. The engineered <italic>S. cerevisiae</italic> strain secreted active PL, which exhibited pectin-degrading activity characterized by significant reductions in residual pectin and increased production of reducing sugars. Since pectin constitutes a major component of coffee mucilage, the secreted PL was applied to coffee beans for mucilage removal. The treated beans presented noticeably reduced residual mucilage, a purer green color, and decreased viscosity. These findings suggest the potential of the engineered <italic>S. cerevisiae</italic> strain for applications in coffee processing, particularly in efficient mucilage removal.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2025},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-5663569/latest.pdf"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-5663569/v1"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"40684030","reference":"Biotechnology letters. 2025 Jul;47(4):78","type":"Preprint of","note":"CrossRef Pre-print loader","orderIn":10001}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2025-04-09","firstPublicationDate":"2025-04-07","firstIndexDate":"2025-04-09","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1001001","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2025-04-07","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"40715925","source":"MED","pmid":"40715925","pmcid":"PMC12297123","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12297123"]},"doi":"10.1186/s11671-025-04316-5","title":"Efficient encapsulation of CRISPR-Cas9 RNP in bioreducible nanogels and release in a cytosol-mimicking environment.","authorString":"Westarp P, Keller T, Brand J, Horvat S, Albrecht K, Beisel C, Groll J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Westarp P","lastName":"Westarp","firstName":"Peter","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-7648-4824"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Functional Materials in Medicine and Dentistry, University of Wuerzburg, Wuerzburg, Germany. peterwerner.westarp@usz.ch."}]}},{"fullName":"Keller T","lastName":"Keller","firstName":"Thorsten","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Functional Materials in Medicine and Dentistry, University of Wuerzburg, Wuerzburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Brand J","lastName":"Brand","firstName":"Jessica","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Functional Materials in Medicine and Dentistry, University of Wuerzburg, Wuerzburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Horvat S","lastName":"Horvat","firstName":"Sonja","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Functional Materials in Medicine and Dentistry, University of Wuerzburg, Wuerzburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Albrecht K","lastName":"Albrecht","firstName":"Krystyna","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Functional Materials in Medicine and Dentistry, University of Wuerzburg, Wuerzburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Beisel C","lastName":"Beisel","firstName":"Chase","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Helmholtz Institute for RNA-Based Infection Research (HIRI), Wuerzburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Groll J","lastName":"Groll","firstName":"Juergen","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Functional Materials in Medicine and Dentistry, University of Wuerzburg, Wuerzburg, Germany. juergen.groll@uni-wuerzburg.de."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-7648-4824"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"20","journalIssueId":3968705,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Discov Nano","title":"Discover nano","essn":"2731-9229","issn":"2731-9229","nlmid":"9918540788706676","isoabbreviation":"Discov Nano"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-07-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"119","abstractText":"CRISPR/Cas9-mediated programmable gene editing has disrupted the biotechnology industry since it was first described in 2012. Safe in vivo delivery is a key bottleneck for its therapeutic use. Viral vector-mediated delivery raises concerns due to immunogenicity, long-term expression, and genomic disruption. Delivery of pre-complexed ribonucleoprotein (RNP) reduces off-target effects, and recombinant Cas9 production is more cost-effective than viral vector synthesis. CRISPR-Cas RNPs do not possess intrinsic cell entry mechanisms, and physical delivery methods are confined to ex vivo editing, necessitating non-viral delivery approaches. Nanogels (NG) are biocompatible polymeric nanoparticles capable of entrapping proteins. Here, we report the first proof of principle that NGs from thiol-functionalized polyglycidol can entrap active RNPs with high efficiency (60 ± 2%). We call these particles CRISPR-Gels. A commercially available E. coli lysate for cell-free transcription and translation (TXTL) was used to mimic the intracellular reductive degradation of NGs while providing a real-time fluorescence readout of RNP activity. Degradation and RNP activity were observed within 30-90 min. The described TXTL assay can be utilized to evaluate the release of RNP in a cytosol-mimicking environment from redox-sensitive nanoparticles in a high-throughput and cost-effective way. Further studies are needed to assess the in vitro and in vivo performance of CRISPR-Gels.","affiliation":"Department of Functional Materials in Medicine and Dentistry, University of Wuerzburg, Wuerzburg, Germany. peterwerner.westarp@usz.ch.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Julius-Maximilians-Universität Würzburg","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Nanoparticle","nanoprecipitation","Crispr","Nanogel","Cas9","Txtl"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s11671-025-04316-5"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12297123"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12297123?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct"]},"dateOfCreation":"2025-07-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-25","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-07-26","firstPublicationDate":"2025-07-26","firstIndexDate":"2025-07-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-11-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"39493894","source":"MED","pmid":"39493894","pmcid":"PMC11530967","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC11530967"]},"doi":"10.34172/bi.2024.30087","title":"CRISPR-Cas9 in basic and translational aspects of cancer therapy.","authorString":"Samareh Salavatipour M, Poursalehi Z, Hosseini Rouzbahani N, Mohammadyar S, Vasei M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Samareh Salavatipour M","lastName":"Samareh Salavatipour","firstName":"Maryam","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2920-9989"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Applied Cell Sciences, School of Advanced Technologies in Medicine, Tehran ‎University of Medical Sciences, Tehran, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Poursalehi Z","lastName":"Poursalehi","firstName":"Zahra","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Applied Cell Sciences, Faculty of Medicine, Urmia University of Medical ‎Sciences, Urmia, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Hosseini Rouzbahani N","lastName":"Hosseini Rouzbahani","firstName":"Negin","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Immunology, Faculty of Medicine, Aja University of Medical Sciences, ‎Tehran, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Mohammadyar S","lastName":"Mohammadyar","firstName":"Sohaib","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Hematology and Laboratory Sciences, Faculty of Allied Medicine, Kerman ‎University of Medical Sciences, Kerman, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Vasei M","lastName":"Vasei","firstName":"Mohammad","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4162-136X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Gene Therapy Research Center, Digestive Disease Research Institute, Tehran University of Medical Sciences, Tehran, Iran."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2920-9989"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4162-136X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"14","journalIssueId":3846588,"dateOfPublication":"2024 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2024,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Bioimpacts","title":"BioImpacts : BI","essn":"2228-5660","issn":"2228-5652","nlmid":"101558125","isoabbreviation":"Bioimpacts"},"printPublicationDate":"2024-01-01"},"pubYear":"2024","pageInfo":"30087","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>The discovery of gene editing techniques has opened a new era within the field of biology and enabled scientists to manipulate nucleic acid molecules. CRISPR-Cas9 genome engineering has revolutionized this achievement by successful targeting the DNA molecule and editing its sequence. Since genomic changes are the basis of the birth and growth of many tumors, CRISPR-Cas9 method has been successfully applied to identify and manipulate the genes which are involved in initiating and driving some neoplastic processes.<h4>Methods</h4>By review of the existing literature on application of CRISPR-Cas9 in cancer, different databases, such as PubMed and Google Scholar, we started data collection for \"CRISPR-Cas9\", \"Genome Editing\", \"Cancer\", \"Solid tumors\", \"Hematologic malignancy\" \"Immunotherapy\", \"Diagnosis\", \"Drug resistance\" phrases. Clinicaltrials.gov, a resource that provides access to information on clinical trials, was also searched in this review.<h4>Results</h4>We have defined the basics of this technology and then mentioned some clinical and preclinical studies using this technology in the treatment of a variety of solid tumors as well as hematologic neoplasms. Finally, we described the progress made by this technology in boosting immune-mediated cell therapy in oncology, such as CAR-T cells, CAR-NK cells, and CAR-M cells.<h4>Conclusion</h4>CRISPR-Cas9 system revolutionized the therapeutic strategies in some solid malignant tumors and leukemia through targeting the key genes involved in the pathogenesis of these cancers.","affiliation":"Department of Applied Cell Sciences, School of Advanced Technologies in Medicine, Tehran ‎University of Medical Sciences, Tehran, Iran.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Drug resistance","Cancer","Immunotherapy","Diagnosis","Solid Tumors","Hematologic Malignancy","Gene Editing","Crispr-cas9"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"doi","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://doi.org/10.34172/bi.2024.30087"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.34172/bi.2024.30087"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC11530967"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC11530967?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":11,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2024-11-04","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-23","electronicPublicationDate":"2024-03-10","firstPublicationDate":"2024-03-10","firstIndexDate":"2024-11-05","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"IND608026245","source":"AGR","title":"Development and application of CRISPR/Cas9 to improve anthocyanin pigmentation in plants: Opportunities and perspectives","authorString":"Mackon E, Jeazet Dongho Epse Mackon GC, Guo Y, Ma Y, Yao Y, Liu P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mackon E","lastName":"Mackon","firstName":"Enerand","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Jeazet Dongho Epse Mackon GC","lastName":"Jeazet Dongho Epse Mackon","firstName":"Guibeline Charlie","initials":"GC"},{"fullName":"Guo Y","lastName":"Guo","firstName":"Yongqiang","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Ma Y","lastName":"Ma","firstName":"Yafei","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Yao Y","lastName":"Yao","firstName":"Yuhang","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Liu P","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Piqing","initials":"P"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"333","journalIssueId":3857056,"dateOfPublication":"2024 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2024,"journal":{"title":"Plant Science.","issn":"0168-9452"},"printPublicationDate":"2024-08-01"},"pubYear":"2024","pageInfo":"Not Available","abstractText":"Since its discovery in 2012, the novel technology of clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats (CRISPR) and CRISPR-associated protein 9 (Cas9) has greatly contributed to revolutionizing molecular biology. It has been demonstrated to be an effective approach for identifying gene function and improving some important traits. Anthocyanins are secondary metabolites responsible for a wide spectrum of aesthetic coloration in various plant organs and are beneficial for health. As such, increasing anthocyanin content in plants, especially the edible tissue and organs, is always a main goal for plant breeding. Recently, CRISPR/Cas9 technology has been highly desired to enhance the amount of anthocyanin in vegetables, fruits, cereals, and other attractive plants with more precision. Here we reviewed the recent knowledge concerning CRISPR/Cas9-mediated anthocyanin enhancement in plants. In addition, we addressed the future avenues of promising potential target genes that could be helpful for achieving the same goal using CRISPR/Cas9 in several plants. Thus, molecular biologists, genetic engineers, agricultural scientists, plant geneticists, and physiologists may benefit from CRISPR technology to boost the biosynthesis and accumulation of anthocyanins in fresh fruits, vegetables, grains, roots, and ornamental plants.","language":"eng","pubModel":"Undetermined","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2024-09-28","dateOfCreation":"2024-09-28","dateOfRevision":"2024-10-06","firstPublicationDate":"2024-08-01","firstIndexDate":"2023-07-12","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42535013","source":"MED","pmid":"42535013","pmcid":"PMC13422435","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13422435"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpls.2026.1846175","title":"Tiny but powerful: protoplast isolation in &lt;i&gt;Solanum melongena&lt;/i&gt; L. and RNPs mediated genome editing.","authorString":"Ferrero M, González MN, Perrone I, Hofvander P, Andersson M, Moglia A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ferrero M","lastName":"Ferrero","firstName":"Martina","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Agricultural, Forest and Food Sciences (DISAFA), University of Torino, Grugliasco, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"González MN","lastName":"González","firstName":"Matías Nicolás","initials":"MN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant Breeding, Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences, Lomma, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Perrone I","lastName":"Perrone","firstName":"Irene","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute for Sustainable Plant Protection, National Research Council (CNR-IPSP), Torino, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Hofvander P","lastName":"Hofvander","firstName":"Per","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1048-2608"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant Breeding, Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences, Lomma, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Andersson M","lastName":"Andersson","firstName":"Mariette","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plant Breeding, Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences, Lomma, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Moglia A","lastName":"Moglia","firstName":"Andrea","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Agricultural, Forest and Food Sciences (DISAFA), University of Torino, Grugliasco, Italy."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1048-2608"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4112080,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Plant Sci","title":"Frontiers in plant science","essn":"1664-462X","issn":"1664-462X","nlmid":"101568200","isoabbreviation":"Front Plant Sci"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1846175","abstractText":"Traditional genetic transformation approaches relying on <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> for the delivery of CRISPR/Cas9 reagents usually provide plants that stably integrate the gene construct in their genome. To meet the EU commission's proposal for a new legislation on plants obtained by new genomic techniques (NGTs), it is important to develop new protocols that produce transgene-free genome edited plants (NGT category 1). Protoplasts are a promising platform, since delivery of CRISPR/Cas9 reagents as ribonucleoproteins (RNPs) is effective in cells lacking their wall. This allows genetic modifications from a transient application, leaving no traces in the recipient genome apart from the desired targeted mutations. With the aim of implementing transgene-free editing of eggplant (<i>Solanum melongena</i> L.), we adapted and improved a protocol previously established in potato and tomato for the isolation of protoplasts from cotyledonary leaves and subsequent CRISPR/Cas9 reagents delivery. Isolated protoplasts were subjected to <i>in vitro</i> culture and regeneration, and the first shoots were regenerated from calli approximately 4-5 months after isolation. Alongside, two transfection protocols were tested for the delivery of RNPs into eggplant protoplasts, one using polyethylene glycol (PEG) in two concentrations (25% and 40%) and one exploiting two formulations of lipofectamines (Lipofectamine CRISPRMAX™ and Lipofectamine™ 3000), all targeting <i>SmChl_H</i> gene, whose inactivation can cause a chlorotic phenotype. Efficient callus regeneration from transfected protoplasts was obtained and the editing efficiency (calculated as the percentage of edited calli on the total of calli that underwent sequencing) was evaluated. 25% PEG treatment provided the highest editing efficiency, and fully edited biallelic calli were retrieved, showing the expected chlorotic phenotype. Even if the efficiency of <i>in vitro</i> regeneration of plants from calli still needs improvement, edited plants were regenerated from protoplasts, representing the first report of RNP mediated genome editing in eggplant protoplasts.","affiliation":"Department of Agricultural, Forest and Food Sciences (DISAFA), University of Torino, Grugliasco, Italy.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Protoplasts","Lipofection","Ribonucleoproteins","Genome Editing","Solanum Melongena L.","Crispr/cas9"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2026.1846175"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13422435"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13422435?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-31","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"38648173","source":"MED","pmid":"38648173","pmcid":"PMC11389839","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC11389839"]},"doi":"10.1093/jxb/erae147","title":"How to use CRISPR/Cas9 in plants: from target site selection to DNA repair.","authorString":"Přibylová A, Fischer L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Přibylová A","lastName":"Přibylová","firstName":"Adéla","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8675-5065"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Plant Biology, Faculty of Science, Charles University, Viničná 5, 12800, Prague 2, Czech Republic."}]}},{"fullName":"Fischer L","lastName":"Fischer","firstName":"Lukáš","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1852-0461"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Plant Biology, Faculty of Science, Charles University, Viničná 5, 12800, Prague 2, Czech Republic."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8675-5065"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1852-0461"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"17","volume":"75","journalIssueId":3827940,"dateOfPublication":"2024 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2024,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Exp Bot","title":"Journal of experimental botany","essn":"1460-2431","issn":"0022-0957","nlmid":"9882906","isoabbreviation":"J Exp Bot"},"printPublicationDate":"2024-09-01"},"pubYear":"2024","pageInfo":"5325-5343","abstractText":"A tool for precise, target-specific, efficient, and affordable genome editing is a dream for many researchers, from those who conduct basic research to those who use it for applied research. Since 2012, we have tool that almost fulfils such requirements; it is based on clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats (CRISPR)/CRISPR-associated protein (Cas) systems. However, even CRISPR/Cas has limitations and obstacles that might surprise its users. In this review, we focus on the most frequently used variant, CRISPR/Cas9 from Streptococcus pyogenes, and highlight key factors affecting its mutagenesis outcomes: (i) factors affecting the CRISPR/Cas9 activity, such as the effect of the target sequence, chromatin state, or Cas9 variant, and how long it remains in place after cleavage; and (ii) factors affecting the follow-up DNA repair mechanisms including mostly the cell type and cell cycle phase, but also, for example, the type of DNA ends produced by Cas9 cleavage (blunt/staggered). Moreover, we note some differences between using CRISPR/Cas9 in plants, yeasts, and animals, as knowledge from individual kingdoms is not fully transferable. Awareness of these factors can increase the likelihood of achieving the expected results of plant genome editing, for which we provide detailed guidelines.","affiliation":"Department of Experimental Plant Biology, Faculty of Science, Charles University, Viničná 5, 12800, Prague 2, Czech Republic.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"The Czech Science Foundation","grantId":"23-07813S","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Plants","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"DNA Repair"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Plants","DNA repair","Cell cycle","Mutagenesis","Cleavage","Editing","Crispr/cas","Post-cleavage Trimming","Staggered Ends"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/jxb/erae147"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC11389839"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC11389839?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":10,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2024-09-11","dateOfCreation":"2024-04-22","dateOfRevision":"2024-09-14","firstPublicationDate":"2024-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2024-04-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR848844","source":"PPR","doi":"10.20944/preprints202405.0100.v1","title":"Advances in Environmental Biotechnology with CRISPR/Cas9: Bibliometric Review and Cutting-Edge Applications","authorString":"Verdezoto-Prado J, Chicaiza-Ortiz C, Mejía-Pérez AB, Freire-Torres C, Viteri-Yánez M, Deng L, Barba-Ostria C, Guaman LP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Verdezoto-Prado J","lastName":"Verdezoto-Prado","firstName":"Jessica","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Chicaiza-Ortiz C","lastName":"Chicaiza-Ortiz","firstName":"Cristhian","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Mejía-Pérez AB","lastName":"Mejía-Pérez","firstName":"Ana Belén","initials":"AB"},{"fullName":"Freire-Torres C","lastName":"Freire-Torres","firstName":"Camila","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Viteri-Yánez M","lastName":"Viteri-Yánez","firstName":"Marco","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Deng L","lastName":"Deng","firstName":"Lirong","initials":"L"},{"fullName":"Barba-Ostria C","lastName":"Barba-Ostria","firstName":"Carlos","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Guaman LP","lastName":"Guaman","firstName":"Linda","initials":"LP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3756-2387"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1125-9128"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3756-2387"}]},"pubYear":"2024","abstractText":"CRISPR/Cas9 has emerged as the predominant method for genome editing due to its cost-effectiveness and broad applicability, playing a crucial role in advancing sustainable practices across various sectors. This systematic review leverages the PRISMA methodology to explore CRISPR/Cas9's impact on environmental protection, thereby supporting the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). Analyzing data from the Web of Science, the review found significant growth in related publications, with a 30% increase since 2014, predominantly from the US, China, Germany, and the UK. The study categorizes the scientific developments into three trends: advancements in agriculture, gene editing techniques, and biofuel production. Key discussions include the use of CRISPR/Cas9 in developing fourth-generation biofuels and environmental biosensors, as well as its applications in enhancing genetic resilience and controlling invasive species. These innovations highlight CRISPR/Cas9's potential in promoting sustainable resource management and energy generation, marking a pivotal contribution to ecological conservation and sustainability efforts.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Preprints.org","yearOfPublication":2024},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"doi","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202405.0100.v1"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202405.0100.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2024-05-09","firstPublicationDate":"2024-05-02","firstIndexDate":"2024-05-09","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR848844","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2024-05-02","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"40981344","source":"MED","pmid":"40981344","pmcid":"PMC12452335","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12452335"]},"doi":"10.3390/dermatopathology12030030","title":"Mimicking the LOX-Related Autosomal Recessive Congenital Ichthyosis Skin Disease Using a CRISPR-Cas9 System and Unravelling 12S-LOX Function in the Skin.","authorString":"Simard-Bisson C, Larochelle S, Moulin VJ, Fruteau de Laclos B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Simard-Bisson C","lastName":"Simard-Bisson","firstName":"Carolyne","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-5187-6344"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre de Recherche du CHU de Québec-Université Laval and Centre de Recherche en Organogénèse Expérimentale de l'Université Laval/LOEX, Quebec, QC G1J 1Z4, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Larochelle S","lastName":"Larochelle","firstName":"Sébastien","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-7046-8266"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre de Recherche du CHU de Québec-Université Laval and Centre de Recherche en Organogénèse Expérimentale de l'Université Laval/LOEX, Quebec, QC G1J 1Z4, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Moulin VJ","lastName":"Moulin","firstName":"Véronique J","initials":"VJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2925-2784"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre de Recherche du CHU de Québec-Université Laval and Centre de Recherche en Organogénèse Expérimentale de l'Université Laval/LOEX, Quebec, QC G1J 1Z4, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, Faculty of Medicine, Université Laval, Quebec, QC G1V 0A6, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Fruteau de Laclos B","lastName":"Fruteau de Laclos","firstName":"Bernard","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre de Recherche du CHU de Québec-Université Laval and Centre de Recherche en Organogénèse Expérimentale de l'Université Laval/LOEX, Quebec, QC G1J 1Z4, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biology, Medical Biochemistry and Pathology, Faculty of Medicine, Université Laval, Quebec, QC G1V 0A6, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2925-2784"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-5187-6344"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-7046-8266"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"12","journalIssueId":4019583,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Dermatopathology (Basel)","title":"Dermatopathology (Basel, Switzerland)","essn":"2296-3529","issn":"2296-3529","nlmid":"101651125","isoabbreviation":"Dermatopathology (Basel)"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-09-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"30","abstractText":"<i>Stratum Corneum</i> (SC) formation in the human epidermis requires lipid processing. Lipoxygenases (LOXs) such as 12R-Lipoxygenase (12R-LOX) and Epidermis-type lipoxygenase 3 (eLOX-3) contribute to this process. Mutations in their genes cause Autosomal Recessive Congenital Ichthyosis (ARCI) in patients. On the other hand, 12S-lipoxygenase (12S-LOX) is expressed in the human epidermis, but its role still remains to be clarified. The involvement of eLOX-3, 12R, and 12S-LOX in conditions or processes such as skin photodamage, wound healing, psoriasis, and atopic dermatitis is suggested but still remains unclear. In order to eventually gain a better understanding of the role of these LOXs in such processes, models of Tissue-Engineered Skins (TESs) with an impaired expression for the native form of either eLOX-3, 12R-LOX, or 12S-LOX were produced using CRISPR-Cas9(D10A) technology. All three models showed impaired keratinocyte differentiation and changes in the prevalence or the size of lipid droplets within the most superficial layers, thus reproducing features observed in ARCI and supporting a role for 12S-LOX in SC formation. Since eLOX-3 and 12R-LOX depleted TES's reproduced features observed in ARCI, such models can be considered as reliable tools for the functional studies of these LOXs in the human epidermis.","affiliation":"Centre de Recherche du CHU de Québec-Université Laval and Centre de Recherche en Organogénèse Expérimentale de l'Université Laval/LOEX, Quebec, QC G1J 1Z4, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Université Laval Research Center, supported by the Fonds de recherche du Québec - Santé (FRQS)","grantId":"1111","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Fondation du CHU de Québec","grantId":"1111","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Quebec Network for Cell, Tissue, and Gene Therapy -ThéCell (a thematic network supported by the Fonds de recherche du Québec-Santé","grantId":"1111","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Epidermis","Lipoxygenase","Tissue engineering","Keratinocytes","Crispr-cas9 Systems"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/dermatopathology12030030"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12452335"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12452335?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gen","uniprot","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-09-22","dateOfCreation":"2025-09-22","dateOfRevision":"2025-09-25","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-09-11","firstPublicationDate":"2025-09-11","firstIndexDate":"2025-09-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-11-28","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42862945","source":"MED","pmid":"42862945","doi":"10.1146/annurev-pathmechdis-032125-013842","title":"Enhancer Hijacking in Hematological Malignancies.","authorString":"Mulet-Lazaro R, Delwel R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mulet-Lazaro R","lastName":"Mulet-Lazaro","firstName":"Roger","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Hematology, Erasmus MC Cancer Institute, University Medical Center Rotterdam, Rotterdam, Netherlands; email: r.muletlazaro@erasmusmc.nl."}]}},{"fullName":"Delwel R","lastName":"Delwel","firstName":"Ruud","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Hematology, Erasmus MC Cancer Institute, University Medical Center Rotterdam, Rotterdam, Netherlands; email: r.muletlazaro@erasmusmc.nl."},{"affiliation":"Oncode Institute, Utrecht, Netherlands; email: h.delwel@erasmusmc.nl."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4275952,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Annu Rev Pathol","title":"Annual review of pathology","essn":"1553-4014","issn":"1553-4006","nlmid":"101275111","isoabbreviation":"Annu Rev Pathol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Cell identity is established by tightly regulated transcriptional programs. Disruption of the regulatory mechanisms governing these programs leads to aberrant behavior and, frequently, cancer. Enhancer hijacking, the process whereby a gene comes under the control of an ectopic enhancer, is a major cause of transcriptional dysregulation. Although recognized since the 1980s, its detection has been traditionally hindered by the low resolution of karyotyping and incomplete knowledge of enhancer biology. This has changed with the development of high-throughput sequencing, leading to a surge in newly identified enhancer hijacking events over the past two decades. Furthermore, functional dissection of enhancers using techniques such as CRISPR/Cas9 has shed light on their mechanistic underpinnings, with acute myeloid leukemia (AML) emerging as a paradigm for this phenomenon. In this review, we outline the historical development of enhancer hijacking, highlight representative examples in hematological malignancies, and discuss the insights derived from this research, particularly in AML.","affiliation":"Department of Hematology, Erasmus MC Cancer Institute, University Medical Center Rotterdam, Rotterdam, Netherlands; email: r.muletlazaro@erasmusmc.nl.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-pathmechdis-032125-013842"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-12","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-12","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"40121081","source":"MED","pmid":"40121081","doi":"10.1016/bs.mie.2025.01.004","title":"Visualizing the conformational landscape of CRISPR-Cas9 through kinetics-informed structural studies.","authorString":"Hibshman GN, Taylor DW.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hibshman GN","lastName":"Hibshman","firstName":"Grace N","initials":"GN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interdisciplinary Life Sciences Graduate Programs, University of Texas at Austin, Austin, TX, United States; Department of Molecular Biosciences, University of Texas at Austin, Austin, TX, United States. Electronic address: grace.hibshman@utexas.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Taylor DW","lastName":"Taylor","firstName":"David W","initials":"DW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interdisciplinary Life Sciences Graduate Programs, University of Texas at Austin, Austin, TX, United States; Department of Molecular Biosciences, University of Texas at Austin, Austin, TX, United States. Electronic address: dtaylor@utexas.edu."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"712","journalIssueId":3918362,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Methods Enzymol","title":"Methods in enzymology","essn":"1557-7988","issn":"0076-6879","nlmid":"0212271","isoabbreviation":"Methods Enzymol"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"41-53","abstractText":"CRISPR-Cas9 has transformed genome editing through its programmability and versatility. Its DNA cleavage activity involves dynamic conformational changes during gRNA binding, DNA recognition, R-loop formation, and endonuclease activation. Understanding these molecular transitions is critical for improving the specificity and efficiency of Cas9, but this remains challenging precisely due to these rapid structural rearrangements. Early structural studies provided foundational insights but were limited to static states under catalytically inactive conditions. Cryo-EM has since enabled visualization of the dynamic nature of active Cas9, by enriching for specific conformations. This chapter introduces a kinetics-informed cryo-EM approach to capture the stepwise activation of Cas9 in real time. With thorough kinetic analyses, such as stopped-flow measurements of R-loop formation, we describe how to identify optimal timepoints to visualize key conformational states with cryo-EM. Integration of kinetic and structural data enables precise mapping of the conformational landscape of Cas9 and other dynamic enzymes, advancing our understanding of their molecular mechanisms and providing a framework for engineering enhanced variants.","affiliation":"Interdisciplinary Life Sciences Graduate Programs, University of Texas at Austin, Austin, TX, United States; Department of Molecular Biosciences, University of Texas at Austin, Austin, TX, United States. Electronic address: grace.hibshman@utexas.edu.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cryoelectron Microscopy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protein Conformation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Kinetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Models, Molecular"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA Cleavage"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbre