lName":"Kar I","lastName":"Kar","firstName":"Indrani","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, Lady Hardinge Medical College, University of Delhi."}]}},{"fullName":"Narendrasinh JD","lastName":"Narendrasinh","firstName":"Jadav Dhruvkumar","initials":"JD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, Pandit Deendayal Upadhyay Medical College, Rajkot."}]}},{"fullName":"Shah SK","lastName":"Shah","firstName":"Sanjay Kumar","initials":"SK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, Janaki Medical College, Janakpur, Nepal."}]}},{"fullName":"Konathala L","lastName":"Konathala","firstName":"Lohini","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Dr NTR University of Health Sciecnes, Vijayawada, Andhra Pradesh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Mohamed N","lastName":"Mohamed","firstName":"Nadine","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, Southern Illinois University, Memorial of Carbondale Hospital, IL."}]}},{"fullName":"Kachhadia MP","lastName":"Kachhadia","firstName":"Meet Popatbhai","initials":"MP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, Pandit Deendayal Upadhyay Medical College, Rajkot."}]}},{"fullName":"Chaudhary MH","lastName":"Chaudhary","firstName":"Mitul Hareshkumar","initials":"MH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, AMA School of Medicine, Makati, Philippines."}]}},{"fullName":"Dave VA","lastName":"Dave","firstName":"Vyapti A","initials":"VA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, Gujarat Medical Education and Research Society, GMERS Valsad, Gujarat."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar L","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Lakshya","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, Pandit Deendayal Upadhyay Medical College, Rajkot."}]}},{"fullName":"Ahmadi L","lastName":"Ahmadi","firstName":"Leeda","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-9589-6527"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, Lady Hardinge medical College, New Delhi."}]}},{"fullName":"Golla V","lastName":"Golla","firstName":"Varshitha","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, International School of Medicine (ISM), Bishkek, Kyrgyzstan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-9589-6527"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"86","journalIssueId":3738221,"dateOfPublication":"2024 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2024,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Ann Med Surg (Lond)","title":"Annals of medicine and surgery (2012)","essn":"2049-0801","issn":"2049-0801","nlmid":"101616869","isoabbreviation":"Ann Med Surg (Lond)"},"printPublicationDate":"2024-03-01"},"pubYear":"2024","pageInfo":"1517-1521","abstractText":"Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a serious health issue that has a significant social and economic impact worldwide. One of the key aetiological signs of the disease is a gradual reduction in cognitive function and irreversible neuronal death. According to a 2019 global report, more than 5.8 million people in the United States (USA) alone have received an AD diagnosis, with 45% of those people falling into the 75-84 years age range. According to the predictions, there will be 15 million affected people in the USA by 2050 due to the disease's steadily rising patient population. Cognitive function and memory formation steadily decline as a result of an irreversible neuron loss in AD, a chronic neurodegenerative illness. Amyloid-beta and phosphorylated Tau are produced and accumulate in large amounts, and glial cells are overactive. Additionally, weakened neurotrophin signalling and decreased synapse function are crucial aspects of AD. Memory loss, apathy, depression, and irritability are among the primary symptoms. The aetiology, pathophysiology, and causes of both cognitive decline and synaptic dysfunction are poorly understood despite extensive investigation. CRISPR/Cas9 is a promising gene-editing technique since it can fix certain gene sequences and has a lot of potential for treating AD and other human disorders. Regardless of hereditary considerations, an altered Aβ metabolism is frequently seen in familial and sporadic AD. Therefore, since mutations in the PSEN-1, PSEN-2 and APP genes are a contributing factor to familial AD, CRISPR/Cas9 technology could address excessive Aβ production or mutations in these genes. Overall, the potential of CRISPR-Cas9 technology outweighs it as currently the greatest gene-editing tool available for researching neurodegenerative diseases like AD.","affiliation":"Department of General Medicine, M. P. Shah medical college, Jamnagar.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Alzheimer’s disease","Gene Editing","Crispr-cas9"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/MS9.0000000000001692"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC10923336"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC10923336?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":4,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2024-03-11","dateOfRevision":"2024-03-12","electronicPublicationDate":"2024-01-05","firstPublicationDate":"2024-01-05","firstIndexDate":"2024-03-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2024-03-13","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42814603","source":"MED","pmid":"42814603","doi":"10.1091/mbc.e26-06-0293","title":"A novel &lt;i&gt;Drosophila&lt;/i&gt; ALP/Enigma protein binds to Alpha-actinin to promote sarcomere assembly.","authorString":"Robinson E, Alvarado Y, Mason S, Tran S, Cripps RM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Robinson E","lastName":"Robinson","firstName":"Ebru","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, San Diego State University, San Diego, CA 92182."}]}},{"fullName":"Alvarado Y","lastName":"Alvarado","firstName":"Yiannis","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, San Diego State University, San Diego, CA 92182."}]}},{"fullName":"Mason S","lastName":"Mason","firstName":"Sarah","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, San Diego State University, San Diego, CA 92182."}]}},{"fullName":"Tran S","lastName":"Tran","firstName":"Shayla","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, San Diego State University, San Diego, CA 92182."}]}},{"fullName":"Cripps RM","lastName":"Cripps","firstName":"Richard M","initials":"RM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, San Diego State University, San Diego, CA 92182."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"37","journalIssueId":4298975,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Biol Cell","title":"Molecular biology of the cell","essn":"1939-4586","issn":"1059-1524","nlmid":"9201390","isoabbreviation":"Mol Biol Cell"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"ar113","abstractText":"Z-discs define sarcomere boundaries and anchor actin filaments in muscle, yet many structural components remain uncharacterized. In this paper, we identify three new members of the Alp/Enigma family in <i>Drosophila</i>, including Uchmaz (CG42319), a PDZ domain-containing protein, that we characterize in detail. We used CRISPR/Cas9 to generate multiple <i>uchmaz</i> knockout alleles, which are viable but result in a flightless phenotype. Mutants exhibit disrupted sarcomere organization in the indirect flight muscles (IFMs), including fragmented and misaligned Z-discs and reduced accumulation of α-Actinin and other Z-disk markers. We find that Uchmaz localizes to the Z-disk, associates with α-Actinin, and is dependent upon α-Actinin for its localization to the Z-disk. These results identify Uchmaz as a component of IFM myofibril architecture required for muscle function. Since a human ortholog of <i>uchmaz</i>, <i>PDLIM2</i>, is associated with severe muscle dysfunction in muscular dystrophies and myofibrillar myopathy, our studies provide insight into conserved mechanisms of Z-disk assembly and its relevance to human muscle disease.","affiliation":"Department of Biology, San Diego State University, San Diego, CA 92182.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"R01 GM124498","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Muscles","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Myofibrils","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Sarcomeres","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drosophila melanogaster","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Microfilament Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Actinin","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Drosophila Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protein Binding"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Actin Cytoskeleton","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"LIM Domain Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Actinin","registryNumber":"11003-00-2"},{"name":"Drosophila Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Microfilament Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"LIM Domain Proteins","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1091/mbc.E26-06-0293"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR800778","source":"PPR","doi":"10.1101/2024.02.03.24302252","title":"Personalized allele-specific CRISPR-Cas9 strategies for myofibrillar myopathy 6","authorString":"Shin JW, Kim K, Lee Y, Choi DE, Lee J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Shin JW","lastName":"Shin","firstName":"Jun Wan","initials":"JW"},{"fullName":"Kim K","lastName":"Kim","firstName":"Kyung-Hee","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"Lee Y","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Yukyeong","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Choi DE","lastName":"Choi","firstName":"Doo Eun","initials":"DE"},{"fullName":"Lee J","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Jong-Min","initials":"J"}]},"pubYear":"2024","abstractText":"Myofibrillar myopathy 6 (MFM6) is a rare childhood-onset myopathy characterized by myofibrillar disintegration, muscle weakness, and cardiomyopathy. The genetic cause of MFM6 is p.Pro209Leu mutation (rs121918312-T) in the BAG3 gene, which generates the disease outcomes in a dominant fashion. Since the consequences of the BAG3 mutation are strong and rapidly progressing, most MFM6 patients are due to de novo mutation. There are no effective treatments for MFM6 despite its well-known genetic cause. Given p.Pro209Leu mutation is dominant, regenerative medicine approaches employing orthologous stem cells in which mutant BAG3 is inactivated offer a promising avenue. Here, we developed personalized allele-specific CRISPR-Cas9 strategies capitalizing on PAM-altering SNP and PAM-proximal SNP. In order to identify the disease chromosome carrying the de novo mutation in our two affected individuals, haplotype phasing through cloning-sequencing was performed. Based on the sequence differences between mutant and normal BAG3 , we developed personalized allele-specific CRISPR-Cas9 strategies to selectively inactivate the mutant allele 1) by preventing the transcription of the mutant BAG3 and 2) by inducing nonsense-mediated decay (NMD) of mutant BAG3 mRNA. Subsequent experimental validation in patient-derived induced pluripotent stem cell (iPSC) lines showed complete allele specificities of our CRISPR-Cas9 strategies and molecular consequences attributable to inactivated mutant BAG3 . In addition, mutant allele-specific CRISPR-Cas9 targeting did not alter the characteristics of iPSC or the capacity to differentiate into cardiomyocytes. Together, our data demonstrate the feasibility and potential of personalized allele-specific CRISPR-Cas9 approaches to selectively inactivate the mutant BAG3 to generate cell resources for regenerative medicine approaches for MFM6.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"medRxiv","yearOfPublication":2024},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.02.03.24302252"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2024-02-05","firstPublicationDate":"2024-02-04","firstIndexDate":"2024-02-05","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42911441","source":"MED","pmid":"42911441","doi":"10.1007/s11274-026-05361-7","title":"High efficient production of adenine in Bacillus subtilis with novel metabolic engineering strategies.","authorString":"Han S, Shen P, Ren H, Xu J, Chen F, Huang J, Zhou M, Xu Z.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Han S","lastName":"Han","firstName":"Shuang","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key-Laboratory of Biomass Chemical Engineering of Ministry of Education, College of Chemical and Biological Engineering, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, 310027, China."},{"affiliation":"Zhejiang Haoqing Biotechnology Co., Ltd, Taizhou, 318000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shen P","lastName":"Shen","firstName":"Peijie","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key-Laboratory of Biomass Chemical Engineering of Ministry of Education, College of Chemical and Biological Engineering, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, 310027, China. pjshen@zju.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Ren H","lastName":"Ren","firstName":"Huijun","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Zhejiang Haoqing Biotechnology Co., Ltd, Taizhou, 318000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu J","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Jiangli","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Zhejiang Haoqing Biotechnology Co., Ltd, Taizhou, 318000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen F","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Feng","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Zhejiang Haoqing Biotechnology Co., Ltd, Taizhou, 318000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang J","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Jun","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Zhejiang Haoqing Biotechnology Co., Ltd, Taizhou, 318000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhou M","lastName":"Zhou","firstName":"Min","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Taizhou Key Laboratory of Drug Biosynthesis, School of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Taizhou University, Taizhou, 318000, China. zhoumin@tzc.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu Z","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Zhinan","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key-Laboratory of Biomass Chemical Engineering of Ministry of Education, College of Chemical and Biological Engineering, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, 310027, China. znxu@zju.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"42","journalIssueId":4294511,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"World J Microbiol Biotechnol","title":"World journal of microbiology & biotechnology","essn":"1573-0972","issn":"1573-0972","nlmid":"9012472","isoabbreviation":"World J Microbiol Biotechnol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"620","abstractText":"Adenine, serves as a crucial precursor for nucleic acid drugs and food additives, currently suffered from limited availability since its high production costs associated with expensive precursors and enzyme preparations. Here, we developed an effective strategy for high-yield adenine production using Bacillus subtilis as the host. Beginning with an adenosine-producing strain, we first knocked-out the apt and adeC genes, which were involved in product degradation pathways, and deleted the purR gene to alleviate the feedback inhibition on purine operon through CRISPR-Cas9 technology. Then, we overexpressed the nucleoside hydrolase RihC, PRPP amidotransferase PurF, and ribokinase RbsK to enhance the de novo adenine biosynthesis and enable the recycling of D-ribose. The production of adenine reached 18 g/L after the fed-batch fermentation, and the titer continued to reach 78 g/L when adenosine was added as the substrate subsequently, revealing the significant catalytic role of RihC produced during the fermentation. This work innovatively integrated de novo biosynthesis and enzymatic conversion strategies to achieve high yield adenine production in Bacillus subtilis, demonstrating a promising scalable process for industrial adenine manufacturing.","affiliation":"Key-Laboratory of Biomass Chemical Engineering of Ministry of Education, College of Chemical and Biological Engineering, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, 310027, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"22578390","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Key Research and Development Program of China","grantId":"2021YFC2103200","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Bacillus subtilis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Purines"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Adenine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BI","qualifierName":"biosynthesis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ribose","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Bacterial Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adenosine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Fermentation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Metabolic Engineering","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Bacillus subtilis","Enzymatic conversion","adenine","De Novo Biosynthesis"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Purines","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Bacterial Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Adenine","registryNumber":"JAC85A2161"},{"name":"Adenosine","registryNumber":"K72T3FS567"},{"name":"Ribose","registryNumber":"681HV46001"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11274-026-05361-7"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-24","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"38299816","source":"MED","pmid":"38299816","pmcid":"PMC10880607","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC10880607"]},"doi":"10.1128/aem.02250-23","title":"Genomic editing in <i>Burkholderia multivorans</i> by CRISPR/Cas9.","authorString":"Ferreira MR, Queiroga V, Moreira LM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ferreira MR","lastName":"Ferreira","firstName":"Mirela R","initials":"MR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9770-6250"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"iBB-Institute for Bioengineering and Biosciences, Instituto Superior Técnico, Universidade de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Associate Laboratory i4HB-Institute for Health and Bioeconomy, Instituto Superior Técnico, Universidade de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Queiroga V","lastName":"Queiroga","firstName":"Vasco","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bioengineering, Instituto Superior Técnico, Universidade de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Moreira LM","lastName":"Moreira","firstName":"Leonilde M","initials":"LM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6838-4245"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"iBB-Institute for Bioengineering and Biosciences, Instituto Superior Técnico, Universidade de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Associate Laboratory i4HB-Institute for Health and Bioeconomy, Instituto Superior Técnico, Universidade de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Department of Bioengineering, Instituto Superior Técnico, Universidade de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9770-6250"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6838-4245"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7812-0472"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"90","journalIssueId":3727428,"dateOfPublication":"2024 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2024,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Appl Environ Microbiol","title":"Applied and environmental microbiology","essn":"1098-5336","issn":"0099-2240","nlmid":"7605801","isoabbreviation":"Appl Environ Microbiol"},"printPublicationDate":"2024-02-01"},"pubYear":"2024","pageInfo":"e0225023","abstractText":"<i>Burkholderia cepacia</i> complex bacteria have emerged as opportunistic pathogens in patients with cystic fibrosis and immunocompromised individuals, causing life-threatening infections. Because of the relevance of these microorganisms, genetic manipulation is crucial for explaining the genetic mechanisms leading to pathogenesis. Despite the availability of allelic exchange tools to obtain unmarked gene deletions in <i>Burkholderia</i>, these require a step of merodiploid formation and another of merodiploid resolution through two independent homologous recombination events, making the procedure long-lasting. The CRISPR/Cas9-based system could ease this constraint, as only one step is needed for allelic exchange. Here, we report the modification of a two-plasmid system (pCasPA and pACRISPR) for genome editing in <i>Burkholderia multivorans</i>. Several modifications were implemented, including selection marker replacement, the optimization of <i>araB</i> promoter induction for the expression of Cas9 and λ-Red system encoding genes, and the establishment of plasmid curing procedures based on the <i>sacB</i> gene or growth at a sub-optimal temperature of 18°C-20°C with serial passages. We have shown the efficiency of this CRISPR/Cas9 method in the precise and unmarked deletion of different genes (<i>rpfR</i>, <i>bceF</i>, <i>cepR</i>, and <i>bcsB</i>) from two strains of <i>B. multivorans</i>, as well as its usefulness in the targeted insertion of the <i>gfp</i> gene encoding the green fluorescence protein into a precise genome location. As pCasPA was successfully introduced in other <i>Burkholderia cepacia</i> complex species, this study opens up the possibility of using CRISPR/Cas9-based systems as efficient tools for genome editing in these species, allowing faster and more cost-effective genetic manipulation.IMPORTANCE<i>Burkholderia</i> encompasses different species of bacteria, some of them pathogenic to animals and plants, but others are beneficial by promoting plant growth through symbiosis or as biocontrol agents. Among these species, <i>Burkholderia multivorans</i>, a member of the <i>Burkholderia cepacia</i> complex, is one of the predominant species infecting the lungs of cystic fibrosis patients, often causing respiratory chronic infections that are very difficult to eradicate. Since the <i>B. multivorans</i> species is understudied, we have developed a genetic tool based on the CRISPR/Cas9 system to delete genes efficiently from the genomes of these strains. We could also insert foreign genes that can be precisely placed in a chosen genomic region. This method, faster than other conventional strategies based on allelic exchange, will have a major contribution to understanding the virulence mechanisms in <i>B. multivorans</i>, but it can likely be extended to other <i>Burkholderia</i> species.","affiliation":"iBB-Institute for Bioengineering and Biosciences, Instituto Superior Técnico, Universidade de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia- Portugal","grantId":"LA/P/0140/2020","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia- Portugal","grantId":"PD/BD/150337/2019","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia-Portugal","grantId":"UIDB/04565/2020","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia- Portugal","grantId":"UIDP/04565/2020","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Burkholderia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Burkholderia cepacia complex","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Burkholderia Infections","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cystic Fibrosis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genomics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Editing"}]},"keywordList":